State machine: 'menu' | 'playing' | 'paused' | 'over'.

Constants:
```
const STEP = 18;
const START_X = -1600;
const GRAVITY = 2400;
const WHEELBASE = 96;
const WHEEL_R = 21;
const MAX_SPEED = 640;
```

Terrain class:
```
const terrain = {
  points: [], // y values at x = START_X + i*STEP
  ramps: [],
  nextRamp: 900,
  lastX: START_X - STEP,
  extendTo(x) {
    while (this.lastX < x) {
      this.lastX += STEP;
      const px = this.lastX;
      // schedule ramps
      if (px > this.nextRamp) {
        const cx = px + 250 + Math.random()*250;
        const amp = 70 + Math.random()*90;
        const width = 100 + Math.random()*90;
        this.ramps.push({cx, amp, width});
        this.nextRamp = cx + 550 + Math.random()*450;
      }
      let y = 0;
      const calm = smoothstep(0, 900, px);
      y += (58*Math.sin(px*0.0015+0.4) + 30*Math.sin(px*0.0038+1.9) + 13*Math.sin(px*0.0091+0.7)) * calm;
      y += 40*Math.sin(px*0.00052+2.2)*smoothstep(300,1400,px);
      for (const r of this.ramps) {
        const d = (px - r.cx)/r.width;
        if (Math.abs(d) < 4) y -= r.amp * Math.exp(-d*d); // negative = up
      }
      this.points.push(y);
    }
  },
  yAt(x) {
    this.extendTo(x + 1200);
    let f = (x - START_X)/STEP;
    let i = Math.floor(f);
    if (i < 0) return this.points[0];
    if (i >= this.points.length-1) return this.points[this.points.length-1];
    const t = f - i;
    return this.points[i]*(1-t) + this.points[i+1]*t;
  },
  slopeAt(x) { return Math.atan2(this.yAt(x+30)-this.yAt(x-30), 60); }
}
```
Note terrain y values: baseline 0; up is negative. Start calm 0. Bike y = terrainY - WHEEL_R.

Ramps: scheduling uses px; ramp cx ahead. When generating new points, ramps array includes all scheduled so far; loop over all ramps each point is O(n) fine (points ~ thousands, ramps ~ tens) but could be O(n*ramps) ~ 100k fine. Only apply if |px-cx| < width*3.

But note: ramp scheduled at px > nextRamp, with cx possibly > px; then earlier points already generated won't have the ramp (it should only affect x >= cx-width*3 > px maybe). Since cx = px+250..., the ramp's influence begins at cx-3w which could be less than px? cx-3w = px+250-300 ≈ px-50, slightly behind. Minor discontinuity; acceptable but could cause a step. To avoid, schedule ramps further ahead: cx = px + 400 + random*200, width ≤ 150 → influence starts px+400-450 = px-50. Still slight. Use cutoff 2.5w. Or compute ramps ahead at init? Simpler: when scheduling, only start influence at x >= schedule point. Actually yAt for x already generated uses stored points, so no retro change. New points at px.. may include ramp with cx-3w < px leading to discontinuity from previous stored point. Use cx = px + 500 + rand*300 and cutoff 2.5*width max 190*2.5=475, so influence starts at px+25 > px. Good.

Ramps should be smooth hills; exp gaussian.

Calm factor means no big hills early. Good.

Ground query for bike uses two wheel x's; function:
```
function groundAt(x, angle) {
  const c = Math.cos(angle)*WHEELBASE*0.5;
  return (terrain.yAt(x-c) + terrain.yAt(x+c))*0.5;
}
```
Hmm when tilted heavily, wheel contact differs vertically. Approx fine.

